  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 158 AM EST Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Center of surface high pressure will remain \nnearly stationary to the southwest of our region, providing us \nwith very quiet weather through this week. Temperatures will be \nabout 10 degrees above seasonal normals during the day, and \nabout 5 degrees above normal at night. Each day we should have \npartly to mostly sunny skies, with mainly clear nights as well. \nA low will pass well north of our region, dragging a cold front \nnorth of our area also. But, could see an increase in clouds \nThursday night into Friday associated with this feature.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Our warm dry pattern will come to a halt by the \nend of the weekend as a coastal system and approaching cold \nfront will drive our weather as this week ends. A robust coastal\nlow is expected to develop off the FL/GA coast this weekend and\nup the coast towards southern New England. Concurrently, a cold\nfront will swing from the Great Lakes eastward towards the St. \nLawrence Sunday. Models remain split on two narrowed solutions, \nthat being a moderate rainfall event for southern/eastern \nVermont and a more drier, light rain event favoring northern New\nYork and areas near the International Border. \n\nIn Scenario 1, the coastal low hugs the east coast, phasing \nwith the cold front Monday with moderate rain across southern \nand eastern Vermont. A sharp precipitation cutoff would likely \noccur with drier air limiting the northward extend of the \nprecipitation. This solution, favored by the GFS ensemble suite \nshows a 30-40% chance for 0.5\" areas south of US-4, and a 10-20%\nchance for around an inch in Windsor County. These \nprobabilities are similar for the Euro ensembles, which compared\nto yesterday is an increase. That said, there are still more \nthan 50% of ensemble members across the Euro and GFS that keep \nthis system more offshore and point us towards scenario 2.\n\nIn Scenario 2, the coastal low tracks more out to sea with \nlittle to no impact to our region, with the only precipitation \ncoming from the weaker cold front. Precipitation Monday would be\nfocused more over northern New York and areas near the \nInternational Border, with perhaps some isolated snow showers in\nthe higher terrain of the Adirondacks and NEK by Monday night \ninto Tuesday. Total precipitation would be elevation dependent \nwith amounts under 0.25\", and many seeing under 0.1.\" This \nscenario is what is currently reflected in the official \nforecast. The forecast will continue to evolve as we get closer \nto the weekend, and especially since we have seen relatively \npoor model predictability with recent coastal systems this \nseason already. Regardless of the precipitation uncertainty, \ntemperatures will fall back towards seasonable norms to start \nnext week, with highs back into the mid 30s to low 40s by \nTuesday.",
